  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 215 PM MDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nWhile model data still differ on the exact location of the ejecting \nupper low, most agree that said Southern Great Basin low digs to near \nthe Four Corners Tuesday night, before lifting out across southwest and \nsouth central Colorado Wednesday, into northeastern Colorado Wednesday \nnight and into the Northern High Plains on Thursday. \n\nThis brings moist southerly flow across south central and southeast \nColorado through the day Wednesday, with widespread showers spreading \nfrom west to east across the region through Wednesday night, as the upper \nlow pivots across northeast Colorado. With colder air associated with the \ntranslating system, snow levels drop to between 8,000-9,000 feet Wednesday\nand especially Wednesday night, leading another 3 to 6 inches of snow \npossible across the higher terrain of the southwest and central mtns \nthrough Wednesday night, as flow aloft becomes more north to northwest \nbehind the parting system. \n\nFurther east across the plains, moist easterly upslope flow Tuesday night\nand early Wednesday looks to develop widespread stratus across plains, \nkeeping temperatures some 15-20F colder than Tuesday, with readings mainly\nin the 50s on Wednesday. Waa showers spread across the region through the \nday, and with the proximity of the upper low, can't rule out a stronger \nstorm or two into Wednesday night, before precipitation winds down Thursday, \nsave for the potential for wrap around precipitation across the central \nMtns into the Pikes Peak region and southeast Mtns Thursday morning and \nafternoon. With the current progged movement of the system, light snow \naccumulations of 1 to 3 inches look to be possible across the higher \nelevations, of Pikes Peak region and into the southeast Mtns Wednesday \nnight and Thursday, with locally higher amounts possible, if the further \nsouth progged location of the GFS turns out to be more correct. Temperatures \nin the 50s to around 60 across the plains on Thursday will be accompanied \nby gusty northerly winds of 20 to 40 mph making for a raw feeling day.  \n\nAs for lows in the 20s and 30s across the higher terrain Wednesday night\ninto Thursday morning, will likely prompt a freeze watch/warning for the \nSan Luis Valley by later shifts, and lows into the 30s across the plains\nThursday night and Friday night leading to possible frost and freeze \nhighlights across portions of the plains, especially northern portions.  \nThose needing to protect sensitive plants and outdoor irrigation should \nstart to take action for the first hint of the end of the growing season. \n\nModel data continues to indicate warmer and drier conditions developing \ninto the weekend, as upper level ridging builds into the region from the \nwest.",
